const e=[{id:"pd-l-who-owns-land",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"Who owns this land, and how would you know",minutes:20,summary:"The register decides ownership in Malaysia. A land search is how an ordinary person reads it, and occupation is not the same thing as title.",plants:["pd-c-ld-01","pd-c-ld-02","pd-c-ld-03","pd-c-ld-04","pd-c-ld-05"],prepares:["pd-p-kampung-title"],reading:[{bookId:"teo-khaw-land-law",where:"the opening chapters on the Torrens system and registration"}],source:"National Land Code (Act 828); standard land office practice.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"The National Land Code was revised and its Act number should be confirmed against a current reprint. Search fees, forms and procedure are set by State land rules and differ between States — confirm with the district land office. Sabah and Sarawak have their own land legislation, so do not carry this straight across.",plate:"land-search",plateCaption:"The answer to “who owns this” is a document. A land search shows the proprietor, the charge and any caveat in one page.",readMinutes:6,sections:[{id:"sec-pd-l-who-owns-land-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Ask who owns a piece of land and most people answer with a family story.",h:"The question people get wrong",body:[{t:"p",text:"Ask who owns a piece of land and most people answer with a story. Grandfather cleared it. The family has been there since before Merdeka."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Mak Cik Zainab's family has farmed the same lot for forty years. Her father cleared it, and died fifteen years ago. Nobody has been to the land office since. Who owns it today?",options:["Her father, because his name is still on the register","Mak Cik Zainab and her brothers and sisters","Nobody, until a court is asked to decide"],answer:0,reveal:"Her father. A man fifteen years dead is still, on paper, the owner. That is the ordinary position of a lot of family land here. If you picked the first answer you reasoned the way the village does. The law reasons from one page instead."},{t:"p",text:"Malaysian land law does not answer with a story. It answers with a register, and the register is kept at the land office."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-who-owns-land-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Under the Code you own land by being registered — the register is the title, not a record of it.",h:"The register is the title",body:[{t:"p",text:"Every piece of registered land in Malaysia has a page in a State register. The person named on that page is the proprietor."},{t:"rule",text:"Under the National Land Code a person becomes the owner of land by being registered as proprietor. The register is the title, not merely evidence of it.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828)"},{t:"p",text:"This arrangement is called the Torrens system. The name matters less than the habit it should give you. Before you believe anything about land, look at the register."},{t:"example",text:"Mak Cik Zainab's family has farmed a lot in Kuala Selangor for forty years. The register still shows her late father as proprietor. In law the family are not the owners, whatever the village knows."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-who-owns-land-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Anyone may order an official search at the district land office; you do not need a lawyer.",h:"How to read a land search",body:[{t:"p",text:"You do not need a lawyer to find out who owns land. Anyone may apply at the district land office for an official search of a lot."},{t:"p",text:"You need the lot number and the mukim or district. The fee is small and the answer comes back on a page or two."},{t:"list",items:["The registered proprietor, with an identity card or company number.","The lot number, the area, and the tenure — held for ever, or for a term of years.","The category of land use: agriculture, building or industry.","Any restriction in interest, which is a condition requiring State consent before a sale or a charge.","Anything registered against the land: a charge to a bank, a lease, or a caveat."]},{t:"p",text:"The certificate the family keeps in a biscuit tin is the issue document of title, often called the geran. It is a copy of the register page, not the ownership itself."},{t:"caution",text:"A search shows one day only. In a purchase a fresh search is done again just before the transfer is handed in for registration."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-who-owns-land-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Long occupation does not turn an occupier into an owner of registered land.",h:"Living on land you do not own",body:[{t:"p",text:"Many Malaysians occupy land they hold no title to. Occupation and ownership are different things, and the Code keeps them apart."},{t:"list",items:["A temporary occupation licence, or TOL, is yearly permission to occupy State land. It is permission, not ownership, and it can end.","A tenant rents. The landlord's name stays on the register throughout.","A family arrangement: one child lives on the land while the title stays in a parent's name."]},{t:"rule",text:"Long occupation does not by itself give title to registered land under the National Land Code. Time alone does not turn an occupier into an owner.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828)"},{t:"caution",text:"This surprises people who have heard of squatter's rights in English law. That doctrine does not run against registered land here. The register decides."},{t:"caution",text:"Some titles carry a further limit. Malay reserved land can only be dealt with within a restricted class of buyers, under State law. Sabah and Sarawak have their own land legislation, so do not carry this lesson straight across."},{t:"compare",title:"Occupying and owning are different things",left:{h:"Occupying",items:["A TOL is yearly permission to occupy State land. Permission, not ownership.","A tenant rents. The landlord's name stays on the register throughout.","A family arrangement: one child lives there, the title stays in a parent's name.","Long occupation does not by itself give title to registered land."]},right:{h:"Owning",items:["The register names the proprietor, and the register is the title.","Ownership comes from registration under the National Land Code.","Time alone does not turn an occupier into an owner.","Squatter's rights from English law do not run against registered land here."]}}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-who-owns-land-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Signing is not owning: a dealing takes effect on registration, and until then you have contract rights.",h:"Signing is not owning",body:[{t:"p",text:"Devi agrees to buy a terrace house in Klang. She signs the sale and purchase agreement and pays the deposit. She has not become the owner."},{t:"rule",text:"A dealing in registered land takes effect only when it is registered. Until then the buyer has rights under the contract, not title.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828)"},{t:"p",text:"The gap between signing and registration can run for months. Almost everything that goes wrong in a land dispute happens inside that gap."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"Where the danger sits in a purchase",caption:"The gap can run for months. Almost everything that goes wrong in a land dispute happens inside it, which is why a fresh search is done just before the transfer goes in.",alt:"Three stages. Devi signs the sale and purchase agreement and pays the deposit, which gives her rights under the contract but not title. Then a gap of months while the transfer is prepared and lodged. Then registration, at which point she becomes the proprietor.",steps:[{label:"Sign, and pay the deposit",note:"rights under the contract, not title"},{label:"The gap",note:"often months — this is where disputes live"},{label:"Registered",note:"now the proprietor"}]}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-who-owns-land-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"If there is land in your family, get the lot number and order a search.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Five cards. Then, if there is any land in your family, do the real thing. Get the lot number and order a search."},{t:"p",text:"The lesson on title by registration takes the same idea further, into what happens when a registration was obtained by fraud."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pd-q-wo1",q:"Who is the owner of registered land in Malaysia?",options:["The person registered as proprietor","The person who has occupied it longest","The person holding the title document","The person who paid for it"],answer:0,why:"The register is the title. Keeping the geran in a drawer is not ownership.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); title is by registration"},{id:"pd-q-wo2",q:"What is the geran, the issue document of title?",options:["A copy of the register page, held by the proprietor","The contract of sale","The ownership itself","A licence to occupy State land"],answer:0,why:"It is a copy. What settles ownership is the register at the land office.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); the issue document of title is a copy of the register"},{id:"pd-q-wo3",q:"What does a restriction in interest on a title mean?",options:["The land cannot be dealt with at all","The land is reserved for agriculture","State consent is needed before the land is sold or charged","The proprietor is under eighteen"],answer:2,why:"It is a condition written on the title. Missing it is a common way for a sale to stall.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); restrictions in interest endorsed on the title"},{id:"pd-q-wo4",q:"A family has farmed land for forty years with no title in their name. What do they own?",options:["The land, by long use","A half share of the land","Nothing in law, unless the register says otherwise","A licence to remain"],answer:2,why:"Long occupation gives no title against the register under the Code.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); long occupation gives no title against the register"},{id:"pd-q-wo5",q:"Devi signs a sale and purchase agreement and pays her deposit. What does she have?",options:["Title to the land","A registered charge","A caveat over the land","Rights under the contract, but not title"],answer:3,why:"A dealing takes effect on registration, and the gap before it is where the risk sits.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); a dealing takes effect on registration"}]},{id:"pd-l-land-paperwork",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"The four papers behind a piece of land",minutes:20,summary:"Transfer, charge, caveat, and the grandfather whose name is still on the title — what each document does, and why speed matters when a signature was forged.",plants:["pd-c-ld-06","pd-c-ld-07","pd-c-ld-08","pd-c-ld-09","pd-c-ld-10"],prepares:["pd-p-kampung-title"],reading:[{bookId:"teo-khaw-land-law",where:"the chapters on dealings, charges and caveats"},{bookId:"salleh-buang-torrens",where:"for the shape of the Torrens machinery, if you want a second account"}],source:"National Land Code (Act 828), including s 340; Small Estates (Distribution) Act 1955; Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read s 340 of the National Land Code itself, including the proviso, in a current reprint. Confirm the current small estates procedure and the value threshold for a small estate — it is set by statute and has been raised. Confirm the citation of Tan Ying Hong in a report you can hold, and check whether anything later has qualified it.",plate:"four-papers",plateCaption:"Four papers, four jobs. Knowing which one is in front of you answers most questions before any law is applied.",readMinutes:7,sections:[{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Most land arguments are arguments about four documents.",h:"Four papers, four jobs",body:[{t:"p",text:"Land arguments are usually arguments about four documents. A transfer, a charge, a caveat, and the paper that moves land on when somebody has died."},{t:"p",text:"Each does one job. Learn what each one does, and most land problems become readable without a textbook."},{t:"table",caption:"Each does one job. Learn what each one does, and most land problems become readable without a textbook.",columns:[{label:"The paper"},{label:"Its one job"}],rows:[["Transfer","Changes the registered owner. The buyer's name replaces the seller's."],["Charge","What a bank gets over the land when it lends against it."],["Caveat","A stop sign on the register."],["The paper for a dead owner","Moves the land on when the owner has died."]]}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A transfer passes ownership when it is registered, not when it is signed.",h:"Transfer: how the name changes",body:[{t:"p",text:"A sale ends with a transfer form signed by the seller and handed in at the land office. The office registers it, and the buyer's name replaces the seller's."},{t:"rule",text:"A transfer of registered land passes ownership when it is registered, not when it is signed.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828)"},{t:"example",text:"Devi's transfer is handed in on a Monday and registered a fortnight later. She became the owner on the day of registration, not on the day she paid."},{t:"p",text:"Stamp duty is paid before registration. Where the title carries a restriction in interest, State consent has to come first."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A charge is security for a debt — and Malaysian law says charge, not mortgage.",h:"Charge: what the bank gets",body:[{t:"p",text:"When a bank lends against land, it does not take ownership. It takes a registered security called a charge."},{t:"rule",text:"A charge is registered against the land as security for a debt. Malaysian law says charge, not mortgage.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828)"},{t:"p",text:"The borrower stays the registered proprietor. The bank appears on the register as chargee, which is a different thing."},{t:"p",text:"If the borrower defaults, the bank serves a notice. If the default is not put right, it applies for an order for sale."},{t:"caution",text:"The bank cannot simply take the house and move in. It has to go through the order for sale process, and the borrower can be heard in it."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A caveat is the stop sign: it freezes dealings while a claim is sorted out.",h:"Caveat: the stop sign",body:[{t:"p",text:"A caveat is a note entered on the register that blocks further dealings while it stands. It is the quickest protection an ordinary person has."},{t:"rule",text:"A private caveat may be entered by a person claiming title to the land, or a registrable interest in it, or a beneficial entitlement under a trust affecting it.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828)"},{t:"p",text:"A caveat does not give you the land. It buys time by stopping anybody else being registered until the claim is sorted out."},{t:"example",text:"Devi hears that her seller is trying to sell the same house to somebody else. She lodges a private caveat that afternoon. The second sale cannot be registered while it stands."},{t:"caution",text:"A caveat lodged without a proper claim is not free. A person who suffers loss because of it can claim compensation from the caveator."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Land in a dead person's name moves only when the distribution is registered.",h:"When the owner has died",body:[{t:"p",text:"This is the most common land problem in Malaysian families. It is a paperwork problem rather than a fight."},{t:"p",text:"Land does not pass to the children by itself. The register keeps showing the dead person until the estate is distributed and that distribution is registered."},{t:"list",items:["A small estate is dealt with at the Land Office, in a distribution hearing the family attends.","Where there is a will, or the estate is larger, the matter goes to court for a grant.","The order or grant is then registered against the title, and only then does the register change."]},{t:"rule",text:"Land registered in a dead person's name moves on only when the distribution of the estate is registered against the title.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); Small Estates (Distribution) Act 1955"},{t:"caution",text:"Delay is expensive. After two generations the heirs have multiplied, some have moved away, and some have died in turn. The cheapest time to do this is now."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Section 340 protects a registered title, and lists the exceptions — fraud, forgery, a void instrument.",h:"When a signature is not real",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sometimes a name reached the register through a forged transfer. The Code does not simply shrug at that."},{t:"rule",text:"A registered title is indefeasible, but s 340 sets out exceptions. Registration obtained by fraud, by forgery, or by a void instrument may be set aside.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828), s 340"},{t:"p",text:"The Federal Court in Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San restored the deferred position. The forger's transferee can be unwound. A later buyer in good faith, for value, is protected."},{t:"caution",text:"That is why speed matters. Once the land has moved on to an innocent buyer, the person who lost it may be left suing for money rather than getting the land back."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-land-paperwork-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The problem is the family case above, with a forged transfer inside it.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Five cards, then the problem question. It is the family case above, with a forged transfer inside it."},{t:"p",text:"The lesson on dealings, charges and caveats goes deeper on each document. This one is only the map."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pd-q-lp1",q:"When does a buyer become the registered owner?",options:["When the agreement is signed","When the price is paid in full","When the transfer is registered","When the keys are handed over"],answer:2,why:"Registration is the moment. Everything before it is contract.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); a transfer takes effect on registration"},{id:"pd-q-lp2",q:"What does a bank get when it lends against land?",options:["Ownership of the land","A registered charge, as security for the debt","A caveat over the land","A lease of the land"],answer:1,why:"Malaysian law says charge, not mortgage, and the borrower stays the proprietor.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); the charge provisions"},{id:"pd-q-lp3",q:"What does a private caveat do?",options:["It blocks further dealings from being registered","It transfers the land to the caveator","It cancels the seller's title","It creates a charge over the land"],answer:0,why:"It buys time; it gives no interest. Lodging one without a proper claim can cost compensation.",source:"National Land Code (Act 828); the caveat provisions"},{id:"pd-q-lp4",q:"A father dies with land in his name. What happens to the register?",options:["It passes to the eldest child at once","It passes to the widow at once","It goes back to the State","Nothing, until the estate is distributed and the distribution registered"],answer:3,why:"Land does not move by itself. The distribution has to be obtained and then registered.",source:"Small Estates (Distribution) Act 1955; National Land Code (Act 828)"},{id:"pd-q-lp5",q:"Where is a small estate usually distributed?",options:["At the Land Office, in a distribution hearing","At the Sessions Court","At the National Registration Department","At the State Secretary's office"],answer:0,why:"Small estates go through the Land Office. Larger ones, or those with a will, go to court.",source:"Small Estates (Distribution) Act 1955"},{id:"pd-q-lp6",q:"A transfer was forged. Is the person registered under it safe?",options:["Yes, because the register is always conclusive","Yes, if a lawyer witnessed the signature","No — forgery is one of the exceptions in s 340","Only if the true owner objects within a year"],answer:2,why:"The exceptions are listed. It is the later innocent buyer for value who is protected, which is why speed matters.",source:"National Land Code s 340; Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC)"}]},{id:"l-leases-tenancies",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"Leases and tenancies, and the line registration draws",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A lease of registered land is a dealing, so it lives on the register and binds whoever buys the land. A short tenancy is exempt from registration, which changes what a buyer of that land ever sees.",source:"National Land Code, the provisions on leases, on tenancies exempt from registration and on endorsement, together with s 340(1) on indefeasibility; Specific Relief Act 1950 on recovery of possession.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for leases, for tenancies exempt from registration or for endorsement on purpose; confirm it in the current National Land Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Confirm the three-year threshold and the maximum lease terms the same way. The rule against self-help recovery of possession is stated without a section number; confirm it in the Specific Relief Act 1950. Section 340(1) is quoted because it is on the verified list. Sabah and Sarawak have their own land legislation, so do not carry this across.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"national-land-code",where:"the parts on leases and tenancies, then the definition of a tenancy exempt from registration"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-leases-tenancies-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two shoplots, two rent agreements, and the Code files them in completely different places.",h:"Two shoplots on the same street",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Halimah rents her shoplot in Ipoh to Encik Kamal. The term is five years. The rent is RM2,400 a month."},{t:"p",text:"Two doors down, Encik Suresh rents an identical shoplot to a baker. That term is two years, at the same rent."},{t:"p",text:"Same street. Same building. Same money. The Code treats the two agreements as different animals."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A buyer purchases both shoplots. She orders a land search on each title. Which of the two rent agreements is she likely to see on the register?",options:["Kamal's five-year agreement","Both, because both are rent agreements","Suresh's two-year agreement"],answer:0,reveal:"The longer one. A lease of registered land is a dealing, so it is registered and it shows. A short tenancy is exempt from registration, so the register may say nothing about it at all. That gap is the whole of this lesson."}]},{id:"sec-l-leases-tenancies-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Length of term decides everything: a lease is registered, and a short tenancy cannot be.",h:"Where the Code draws the line",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Code does not ask what the parties called their document. It asks how long the term is."},{t:"p",text:"Above a short threshold the arrangement is a lease. A lease is one of the dealings the Code allows, and it takes effect on registration."},{t:"rule",text:"A tenancy for a term not exceeding three years is a tenancy exempt from registration under the National Land Code, and it cannot be registered. An arrangement granting possession for a longer term must be created as a registered lease.",source:"National Land Code, the provisions on leases and on tenancies exempt from registration. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"table",caption:"Lease and short tenancy compared, on the points that decide a real dispute",columns:["Question","Registered lease","Tenancy exempt from registration"],rows:[["How long is the term?","Longer than the short statutory threshold","Not exceeding three years"],["Does it go on the register?","Yes, as a registrable dealing","No, it cannot be registered"],["When does it take effect?","On registration","On the agreement, by contract"],["Can a buyer of the land see it?","Yes, on a land search","Only from an endorsement, or from the tenant being there"]],alt:"A table comparing a registered lease with a short tenancy. A lease runs longer than the statutory threshold. It is a registrable dealing, it takes effect on registration, and it shows on a land search. A short tenancy runs for not more than three years and cannot be registered. It takes effect by contract. A buyer sees it only from an endorsement, or from the tenant being visibly there."},{t:"caution",text:"The three-year line is the one figure in this lesson. Confirm it in the current Code before you rely on it. Note too that the Code caps how long a lease may run."},{t:"p",text:"That cap is not the same for the whole of a lot and for part of one. This lesson does not give those figures. Look them up."}]},{id:"sec-l-leases-tenancies-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A registered lease is an interest in the land itself; a short tenancy leans on contract and on being seen.",h:"What registration buys the tenant",body:[{t:"p",text:"Registration is not paperwork for its own sake. It changes what the tenant owns."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 340(1) protects the title or interest of any person for the time being registered as proprietor, and of any person in whose name a lease, charge or easement is registered. A registered lease is therefore indefeasible on the same terms as a registered title.",source:"National Land Code, s 340(1)."},{t:"p",text:"So Kamal holds an interest in the land itself. If Halimah sells the shoplot, the buyer takes it with Kamal's lease already on the register."},{t:"p",text:"Suresh's baker is weaker. The tenancy is a contract with Suresh, and it cannot be put on the register at all."},{t:"p",text:"The Code answers that with an endorsement. A tenancy exempt from registration can be noted on the register, so a buyer searching the title sees it."},{t:"caution",text:"A tenant who does nothing is relying on two things. One is the contract. The other is that a careful buyer will notice somebody trading from the shop and ask why."},{t:"p",text:"Raise this before signing, not after. Ask whether the tenancy will be endorsed, and ask who pays for that."}]},{id:"sec-l-leases-tenancies-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A landlord who wants possession back goes to court; changing the locks is not a remedy.",h:"Getting the land back",body:[{t:"p",text:"A lease or tenancy ends in three ordinary ways. The term runs out, the parties agree to end it, or the tenant breaks a term."},{t:"list",items:["**The term runs out.** Nothing more is needed, though a registered lease still has to come off the register.","**Surrender.** The parties agree to end it early, and a registered lease is again dealt with on the register.","**Forfeiture for breach.** The landlord serves notice of the breach first, and gives a chance to put it right."]},{t:"p",text:"What a landlord may not do is take the shop back by hand. That is the rule that surprises people most."},{t:"rule",text:"A person in possession of immovable property may not be dispossessed except by due process of law. Self-help recovery of possession is not permitted, and possession is recovered through the court.",source:"Specific Relief Act 1950, the provisions on recovery of possession. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"caution",text:"Changing the locks, cutting the water, or carting out the tenant's stock is unlawful. It also hands the tenant a claim, which is the opposite of what the landlord wanted."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Kamal stops paying rent. Halimah changes the locks on the shoplot early one Sunday. Where does that leave her?",options:["She has lawfully forfeited the lease, because the rent was unpaid","She has acted unlawfully, and Kamal may sue to be let back in","She is safe, because the lease was registered in Kamal's name"],answer:1,why:"Unpaid rent may well justify forfeiture. It does not justify taking possession by hand. Possession is recovered through the court, and self-help gives Kamal a claim of his own. Notice how the two questions separate. Whether Halimah has a right, and how she may enforce it, are not the same question."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-co-proprietorship",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"Owning land with other people",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Two or more people on one title hold undivided shares, and an undivided share is not a piece of ground. Nothing in the Code lets one of them fence off a corner and call it theirs.",source:"National Land Code, the provisions on co-proprietorship, on partition and on sale in lieu of partition, together with s 323 on private caveats.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for co-proprietorship, for partition or for sale in lieu of partition on purpose; confirm it in the current National Land Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Section 323 is quoted because it is on the verified list. Whether an heir of an undistributed estate holds a caveatable interest has been litigated more than once, so check the current position before advising. Sabah and Sarawak have their own land legislation, so do not carry this across.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"national-land-code",where:"the parts on co-proprietorship and partition, then s 323 on who may enter a private caveat"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-co-proprietorship-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Three siblings, one lot in Kuala Pilah, and a brother who wants to build on his third.",h:"Three names on one title",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suriani, Faizal and Nurul are on one title in Kuala Pilah. The lot is two acres. It came to them from their late father's estate."},{t:"p",text:"The register shows each of them with an undivided one-third share."},{t:"p",text:"Faizal wants to build a house at the back of the lot. He says the back third is his."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Faizal fences off the back third of the lot and starts building. Is he entitled to?",options:["Yes, because he owns a third of the land","Only if his share is the largest of the three","No, because his third is a share of the whole lot and not a part of the ground"],answer:2,reveal:"His share is a fraction of the whole, not a marked-out piece. Until the land is partitioned, no co-proprietor can point at any part of the ground and call it theirs. If you thought a third meant a third of the field, that is the instinct the word undivided exists to correct."}]},{id:"sec-l-co-proprietorship-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Each share is a fraction of the whole lot, and a dead co-proprietor's share falls into the estate.",h:"Undivided shares, and no survivorship",body:[{t:"p",text:"The word to hold on to is undivided. A share is a fraction of the entire lot, from the front fence to the back."},{t:"rule",text:"Where land is held under the National Land Code by two or more persons, they hold as co-proprietors in undivided shares. No co-proprietor is entitled to any defined part of the land, and each is entitled to possession of the whole.",source:"National Land Code, the provisions on co-proprietorship. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"p",text:"There is a second consequence, and it catches people trained on English land law."},{t:"rule",text:"The Code does not provide for a right of survivorship between co-proprietors. On the death of a co-proprietor, the share forms part of that person's estate and passes under it.",source:"National Land Code, the provisions on co-proprietorship, read with the law on the distribution of estates. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"p",text:"So Nurul's third does not fall to her brother and sister when she dies. It goes into her estate, and her own heirs take it."},{t:"table",caption:"What each co-proprietor has, and what none of them has",columns:["The claim","The answer"],rows:[["A defined piece of the ground","No. Shares are undivided until the lot is partitioned."],["A right to be on the land","Yes. Each co-proprietor may possess the whole of it."],["A right to keep the others out","No. Possession of the whole belongs to all of them."],["A share that passes on death","Yes, through the estate. There is no survivorship."]],alt:"A table of four claims. A defined piece of ground: no, shares are undivided until partition. A right to be on the land: yes, each co-proprietor may possess the whole. A right to keep the others out: no, they all possess the whole. A share that passes on death: yes, through the estate, because there is no survivorship."}]},{id:"sec-l-co-proprietorship-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"You may sell or charge your own share alone; the whole lot needs every co-proprietor.",h:"What one co-proprietor may do alone",body:[{t:"p",text:"A share is property. Faizal can sell his third, and a bank can take a charge over it."},{t:"list",items:["**Your own share.** You may transfer it, or charge it, without asking the others.","**The whole lot.** A transfer, lease or charge of the whole needs all the co-proprietors.","**The ground itself.** Nobody may fence a part off, or shut the others out of it.","**Rent collected.** A co-proprietor who takes the whole rent can be made to account to the rest."]},{t:"caution",text:"Selling a share is lawful and still hard. Few buyers want a one-third share of a lot with two strangers on the title, and the price shows it."},{t:"p",text:"There is a quieter risk. A share can be charged, and a charge on one share can end in an order for sale of that share."},{t:"p",text:"The others can protect their position on the register instead of arguing later."},{t:"rule",text:"A private caveat may be entered by a person claiming title to the land, or claiming any registrable interest in it, or claiming to be beneficially entitled under any trust affecting it.",source:"National Land Code, s 323."},{t:"p",text:"That matters most before an estate is distributed. An heir is not yet on the register, and a caveat is a common way to hold the claim in place."},{t:"caution",text:"Whether an heir of an undistributed estate has a caveatable interest has been fought over more than once. Check the current position before you advise anyone to lodge one."}]},{id:"sec-l-co-proprietorship-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Partition turns shares into separate titles; where it cannot be done, the answer is a sale.",h:"Splitting up, or getting out",body:[{t:"p",text:"The clean answer to Faizal's problem is partition. Partition ends co-proprietorship by cutting the lot into separate titles."},{t:"p",text:"After partition each of them holds a lot of their own, with a title of their own. Only then can Faizal build at the back."},{t:"list",items:["Partition is applied for at the land office, under the procedure the Code lays down.","Each resulting lot has to satisfy the Code's requirements, including any minimum area.","Whose consent is needed, and when partition may be refused, are set out in the Code.","Where partition is not practicable, the route is a sale of the whole land instead."]},{t:"caution",text:"Do not leave this to the next generation. Three co-proprietors become nine, then twenty, and every one of them has to be found and has to sign."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Suriani, Faizal and Nurul cannot agree. Faizal wants a title of his own over the back of the lot. What does he need?",options:["Partition of the lot, so that a separate title issues","A private caveat over the back third","Nothing, because a one-third share already gives him a third of the ground"],answer:0,why:"A caveat only freezes the register, and it hands over no piece of ground. His share stays undivided until the lot is partitioned. Partition is the procedure that turns a share of the whole into a lot of his own, and it is applied for at the land office."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-strata",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"Strata title, and what you own in a condominium",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A condominium buyer owns a parcel and an undivided share of the common property, measured in share units. Almost every argument in a strata building starts at that one division.",source:"Strata Titles Act 1985; Strata Management Act 2013; National Land Code, s 340 on indefeasibility and the parts on subdivision of buildings.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for parcels, common property, share units, accessory parcels, management bodies or the recovery of charges on purpose; confirm it in the current Strata Titles Act 1985 and Strata Management Act 2013 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. This lesson also states no section number for the National Land Code's provisions on subdivision of buildings on purpose; confirm it in the current National Land Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Section 340 is named because it is on the verified list, and nothing else here carries a number. The Tribunal's monetary limit is deliberately not stated. Sabah and Sarawak have their own strata legislation, so do not carry this across.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"national-land-code",where:"the part on subdivision of buildings, then read the Strata Titles Act 1985 alongside it"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-strata-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Puan Meera has keys, a car park bay and a lift she uses daily, and she does not own all three.",h:"Keys to unit B-12-3",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Meera buys a condominium unit in Cheras for RM480,000. She gets keys to unit B-12-3 and a car park bay downstairs."},{t:"p",text:"She uses the lift every day and the pool on Sundays. The corridor outside her door is swept by somebody she has never met."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Meera owns unit B-12-3. Which of these does she own outright, on her own?",options:["The unit only, plus an undivided share of everything that is shared","The unit, the corridor and her share of the lift","The unit and the car park, but not the corridor"],answer:0,reveal:"The parcel is hers. Everything shared is common property, and she holds an undivided share of it with every other owner. The car park is usually an accessory parcel, tied to her unit and not sellable apart from it. Almost every strata argument begins at that line."}]},{id:"sec-l-strata-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Parcels and common property split the building; share units then measure money and votes.",h:"Parcel, common property, share units",body:[{t:"p",text:"A strata scheme divides one building on one lot into many titles. The division is done by survey and recorded in a strata plan."},{t:"rule",text:"Under the Strata Titles Act 1985 a building is divided into parcels and common property. A parcel proprietor holds a strata title to the parcel, together with an undivided share in the common property. The size of that share is expressed in share units.",source:"Strata Titles Act 1985. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"table",caption:"The vocabulary of a strata scheme, and what each word actually owns",columns:["Word","What it means"],rows:[["Parcel","The unit itself. Meera holds a strata title to it."],["Common property","Everything outside the parcels: corridors, lifts, roof, pool, drains."],["Accessory parcel","A car park or store tied to a parcel. It cannot be sold on its own."],["Share unit","The measure of a parcel's share, its charges and its vote."],["Management corporation","The body of all parcel proprietors, which holds the common property."]],alt:"Five strata words. A parcel is the unit itself, held under a strata title. Common property is everything outside the parcels, such as corridors, lifts, roof, pool and drains. An accessory parcel is a car park or store tied to a parcel and not sellable on its own. A share unit measures a parcel's share, its charges and its vote. The management corporation is the body of all proprietors, and it holds the common property."},{t:"p",text:"Share units do more work than anything else in the scheme. They set what Meera pays each month, and how much her vote is worth."},{t:"caution",text:"Read the schedule of share units before buying. A large parcel carries more share units, so it carries a larger share of every bill the building runs up."}]},{id:"sec-l-strata-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Management passes from developer to joint management body to management corporation, in that order.",h:"Who runs the building",body:[{t:"p",text:"Somebody has to sweep the corridor and service the lift. Malaysian law hands that job on in stages."},{t:"list",items:["**The developer** manages first, from vacant possession until a joint management body is formed.","**The joint management body** is formed at a meeting of buyers, and runs the building before strata titles issue.","**The management corporation** comes into being once strata titles issue, and takes over at the first annual general meeting."]},{t:"rule",text:"The Strata Management Act 2013 governs the management of stratified property. It covers the maintenance account and the sinking fund, the by-laws, and the recovery of charges. Contributions are payable in proportion to the share units of each parcel.",source:"Strata Management Act 2013. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"p",text:"Two funds matter. The maintenance account pays the day-to-day bills. The sinking fund pays for the big repairs that come later."},{t:"caution",text:"Arrears are not a private matter between one owner and the management. The Act gives the management body ways to recover them, and an owner in arrears can lose the right to vote."},{t:"p",text:"By-laws bind everyone in the building. Some of them are prescribed by the Act, and the owners may add their own by a proper resolution."}]},{id:"sec-l-strata-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Late strata titles leave a buyer with contract rights only, sometimes for years.",h:"Where strata goes wrong",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two problems come up more than any others. Charges go unpaid, and strata titles are late."},{t:"p",text:"The second one bites hardest. A buyer can take vacant possession years before a strata title issues for the parcel."},{t:"p",text:"Until then Meera is not the registered proprietor of anything. She holds rights under her sale and purchase agreement, against the developer."},{t:"p",text:"Once the strata title issues and is registered in her name, she is a registered proprietor. Section 340 then protects her parcel as it protects any other registered title."},{t:"caution",text:"The Strata Management Tribunal hears many strata disputes cheaply, and it is meant to be used without a lawyer. It has a monetary limit, and some disputes belong in court instead."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Meera took vacant possession two years ago. No strata title has issued yet. What does she hold?",options:["A registered title to the parcel","Rights under her agreement with the developer, but no registered title","An undivided share of the master title, registered in her name"],answer:1,why:"Registration is what makes a proprietor, and there is no strata title to register yet. Until it issues she holds contractual rights against the developer. This is the same gap you met between signing and registration in an ordinary sale, stretched out over years."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-land-acquisition",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"When the State takes your land",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"The State can take registered land compulsorily, and an indefeasible title is no answer to it. What an owner can usually fight about is not the taking but the money.",source:"Land Acquisition Act 1960; the Federal Constitution's guarantee against deprivation of property without adequate compensation; National Land Code, on registered title generally.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the acquisition purposes, the declaration, the enquiry, the award or the reference to court on purpose; confirm it in the current Land Acquisition Act 1960 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. The constitutional guarantee is stated without an article number for the same reason; confirm it in the Federal Constitution as published by the Attorney General's Chambers. This lesson also states no section number for the National Land Code on purpose; confirm it in the current National Land Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Sabah and Sarawak have their own land and acquisition legislation, so do not carry this across.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"national-land-code",where:"the parts on registered title, then read the Land Acquisition Act 1960 alongside them"},{statuteId:"federal-constitution",where:"the fundamental liberties Part, on deprivation of property and adequate compensation"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-land-acquisition-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A clean title, no charge, no caveat, and a notice on the fence that ends the argument.",h:"A notice on the fence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Ganesan's family has held a lot in Seberang Perai for thirty years. The register is clean. There is no charge and no caveat."},{t:"p",text:"One morning a notice is nailed to the fence. The land is wanted for a road."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Ganesan is the registered proprietor, and his title is indefeasible. Does that stop the State taking the land?",options:["No, because compulsory acquisition works whatever the register says","Yes, because an indefeasible title cannot be taken away","Only if he lodges a caveat quickly enough"],answer:0,reveal:"Indefeasibility protects you against other people. It is no answer to the State using a statutory power to acquire. What the law gives Ganesan instead is process and compensation. If you assumed a clean title was a shield, this is the lesson that fixes that."}]},{id:"sec-l-land-acquisition-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The State Authority acquires, and the Constitution demands law and adequate compensation.",h:"The power, and what it is for",body:[{t:"p",text:"Land is a State matter. It is the State Authority, not the Federal Government, that acquires land in a State."},{t:"rule",text:"Land may be compulsorily acquired by the State Authority under the Land Acquisition Act 1960 for the purposes that Act names. Those include a public purpose, and a purpose the State Authority is satisfied is beneficial to the economic development of the country.",source:"Land Acquisition Act 1960. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"p",text:"The Federal Constitution sits above the Act. It forbids deprivation of property except in accordance with law, and it requires adequate compensation."},{t:"rule",text:"No person shall be deprived of property save in accordance with law, and no law shall provide for the compulsory acquisition or use of property without adequate compensation.",source:"Federal Constitution, the guarantee on deprivation of property. No article number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"caution",text:"The economic development purpose is the controversial one. Land can be taken from a family and end up in the hands of a company, and the Act permits that on its own terms."},{t:"p",text:"Notice what the Constitution does and does not do. It insists on law, and on adequate compensation. It does not give you a right to keep the land."}]},{id:"sec-l-land-acquisition-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Notification, gazetted declaration, enquiry, award, possession, and the declaration is hard to attack.",h:"How an acquisition runs",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Act runs in a fixed order. Knowing the order tells you where you still have something worth saying."},{t:"table",caption:"The stages of a compulsory acquisition, and what happens at each one",columns:["Stage","What happens"],rows:[["Preliminary notification","The State signals that land in the area may be needed. Surveys may follow."],["Declaration in the Gazette","The State declares that the land is needed for the stated purpose."],["Enquiry","The Land Administrator hears evidence on value and on who is entitled."],["Award","Compensation is awarded, and the award is served on the persons interested."],["Possession and vesting","The State takes possession, and the land vests in the State Authority."]],alt:"Five stages of a compulsory acquisition. First a preliminary notification that land in an area may be needed, which surveys may follow. Then a declaration in the State Gazette that the land is needed for the stated purpose. Then an enquiry before the Land Administrator into value and entitlement. Then an award of compensation, served on the persons interested. Last, possession is taken and the land vests in the State Authority."},{t:"rule",text:"A declaration published in the Gazette is treated by the Act as conclusive evidence that the land is needed for the purpose stated in it.",source:"Land Acquisition Act 1960. No section number is stated here on purpose."},{t:"p",text:"Read that rule twice. It is why arguing that the road is unnecessary almost never works."},{t:"caution",text:"Attend the enquiry, and bring evidence of value. A person who ignores the enquiry throws away the cheapest chance to be heard about money."},{t:"p",text:"The people paid are the persons interested, and that is wider than the proprietor. A registered chargee or lessee has a claim on the compensation too."}]},{id:"sec-l-land-acquisition-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Market value at the date of the declaration, plus named heads, then a reference to the High Court.",h:"Compensation, and how to fight about it",body:[{t:"p",text:"Compensation is built on market value, taken at the date of the declaration. What the land is worth years later is not the question."},{t:"list",items:["**Market value** of the land at the date of the declaration in the Gazette.","**Severance**, where taking part of a lot damages the value of the rest.","**Injurious affection**, where the works themselves harm the land left behind.","**Removal or relocation costs**, where the taking forces a move.","**Not** an extra allowance simply because the taking was compulsory."]},{t:"p",text:"One more exclusion is worth remembering. A rise in value caused by the scheme itself is not paid for."},{t:"p",text:"If the award is too low, the owner objects and asks for a reference to the High Court. The judge hears it sitting with assessors who know land values."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Ganesan thinks the award for his lot is far too low. What is his route?",options:["An objection at the enquiry, then a reference to the High Court on the amount","Judicial review of the declaration, on the ground that the road is unnecessary","A private caveat, to stop the land vesting in the State"],answer:0,why:"The declaration is treated as conclusive that the land is needed, so attacking the purpose almost never works. The amount is a different question, and the Act gives a route for it. Object at the enquiry, then ask for a reference heard by a judge sitting with assessors. A caveat does nothing against a statutory power."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-torrens",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"Title by registration, and the limits of indefeasibility",minutes:30,summary:"The Torrens system, section 340 of the National Land Code, and the case that had to be overruled before the section worked as it was meant to.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"teo-khaw-land-law",where:"the chapters on indefeasibility and on dealings"},{bookId:"salleh-buang-torrens",where:"for the Torrens principles and the system's history"}],source:"National Land Code s 340; Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC); Civil Law Act 1956 s 6.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"The National Land Code was revised and its Act number should be confirmed before citing — the reading list itself flags this. Read s 340 in the current reprint, and confirm the Tan Ying Hong citation. Anything written before 2010 on indefeasibility must be read with Adorna Properties in mind.",plate:"register",plateCaption:"Title by registration, not registration of title. The register is not a record of a title acquired elsewhere; it is the thing that creates it.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-torrens-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Forty years of farming, a name on a title, and a document in a drawer — what actually makes it his?",h:"Land in a kampung",body:[{t:"p",text:"Pak Din has farmed the same two acres outside Kuala Selangor for forty years. His name is on the title at the land office, and the document itself lives in a tin under his bed."},{t:"p",text:"One morning a stranger arrives with papers and says the land is his. He has a title too, in his own name, issued by the same land office. Only one of the two of them can be right."},{t:"p",text:"Malaysian land law has a hard and clear rule about which. Understanding why the rule is so hard is the whole of this lesson."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Pak Din has farmed the land for forty years and has a title in his name. The stranger has a title in his name too, from the same land office. Before you read on — who does the law say owns it?",options:["Pak Din, because forty years of working it must count for something","Whichever of them the register at the land office names now","Neither, until a court decides between the two documents"],answer:1,reveal:"The register decides, and it decides on its own. Not the years. Not the tin under the bed. Not who seems more deserving. If your instinct was the first answer, hold on to that feeling. It is the instinct this system was built to overrule. The rest of the lesson is about what that costs."}]},{id:"sec-l-torrens-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Title by registration, not registration of title: the register creates the interest rather than recording it.",h:"The register is the title",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysian land law runs on a system called **Torrens**, named after the man who designed it. One single idea organises everything else in the subject."},{t:"p",text:"The register kept at the land office is not evidence of who owns the land. It *is* the ownership. A person becomes the owner by being registered, and not by holding a good bundle of old papers that registration then writes down."},{t:"rule",text:"Title by registration, not registration of title. The register is conclusive, and a purchaser deals with the register rather than investigating the history behind it.",source:"National Land Code; the Torrens principle"},{t:"p",text:"The point of arranging things that way is to make buying land cheap and safe. A buyer checks the register and stops there, instead of paying somebody to trace deeds back through fifty years and three families."},{t:"p",text:"Everything difficult in this subject comes from the cases where that promise collides with a real victim of fraud or forgery."},{t:"caution",text:"Section 6 of the Civil Law Act 1956 shuts English land law out, and this is the reason why. English doctrines about equitable interests and notice would cut across the conclusiveness of the register, and that conclusiveness is the whole mechanism."}]},{id:"sec-l-torrens-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 340 promises an indefeasible title, then lists the exceptions that qualify the promise.",h:"Section 340: the promise",body:[{t:"p",text:"The promise the Code makes is that a registered owner cannot be shifted. The technical word for that is **indefeasible**, and indefeasibility simply means a registered title that cannot be set aside."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 340(1): the title or interest of any person for the time being registered as proprietor, or in whose name any lease, charge or easement is registered, is indefeasible.",source:"National Land Code, s 340(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: whoever is on the register owns it, and that goes for a lease, a charge or an easement registered against the land as well."},{t:"p",text:"Subsection (2) then sets out the exceptions, and the list is short and closed. Indefeasibility is defeated in three situations."},{t:"list",items:["The registration was obtained by fraud or misrepresentation, and the registered owner was a party to it or knew of it.","The registration was obtained by forgery, or by an instrument that was void or insufficient.","The title was unlawfully acquired in the purported exercise of a power given by written law."]},{t:"p",text:"Then comes the proviso, and the whole subject turns on it. A title that could be set aside under subsection (2) is still protected once it reaches somebody who bought it in good faith and paid for it."},{t:"p",text:"**In good faith** means honestly, and without knowing about the fraud. **Valuable consideration** means the buyer actually paid something, rather than being handed the land as a gift."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A forger transfers Pak Din's land into his own name. Is the forger's registered title safe?",options:["Yes — once registered, a title cannot be touched","Yes, if the land office accepted the documents","No — forgery is one of the exceptions in section 340"],answer:2,why:"Section 340 gives an indefeasible title, then takes it back in the listed cases, and forgery is one of them. The person registered *by* the forgery is not protected. What happens to someone who later buys from him is a different question, and it is the one the next section is about."}]},{id:"sec-l-torrens-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Deferred indefeasibility: the immediate transferee under a forgery loses; the next honest buyer does not.",h:"What that means in practice",body:[{t:"example",text:"Somebody forges Pak Din's signature and transfers the land to Ah Kow, who is in on the scheme. Ah Kow is registered as the owner. He then sells the land on to Salmah for RM180,000. Salmah knows nothing about the forgery and pays the full asking price."},{t:"example",text:"Ah Kow's title can be undone, because he is the immediate transferee under a forged instrument. Salmah's title cannot be touched at all. Pak Din loses the land, and has to chase Ah Kow for money instead."},{t:"rule",text:"Deferred indefeasibility: the immediate registered proprietor whose registration was procured by fraud or forgery takes a defeasible title. A subsequent bona fide purchaser for value takes an indefeasible one.",source:"National Land Code, s 340(2) and the proviso to s 340(3)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the forger's buyer can be unwound, but the next innocent buyer down the line cannot. The line is drawn one transaction later than a first reading of the section suggests."},{t:"p",text:"There is a reason for drawing it there. The innocent buyer is exactly the person the register exists to protect, and protecting her is what makes the register worth relying on."},{t:"caution",text:"This is hard on Pak Din, and it is meant to be a trade-off rather than a kindness. The system buys cheap and certain conveyancing at the price of leaving some genuine victims with a claim for money instead of their land."}]},{id:"sec-l-torrens-4",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Tan Ying Hong overruled Adorna Properties, and nothing in the earlier judgment says it was wrong.",anchor:"fig-l-torrens-0",h:"The case that had to be overruled",body:[{t:"p",text:"This is the best example in Malaysian law of the doctrine of precedent doing real work. It is worth knowing as a piece of history, quite as much as for the rule itself."},{t:"p",text:"In Adorna Properties the Federal Court read section 340 as giving **immediate** indefeasibility. On that reading even Ah Kow, the first transferee under the forged instrument, ended up with a title nobody could touch."},{t:"p",text:"The consequence was that a registered owner could lose their land to a forger's buyer and have no remedy at all. That turned the protective purpose of the exceptions upside down."},{t:"rule",text:"Tan Ying Hong overruled Adorna Properties and restored deferred indefeasibility: a registration obtained by forgery is defeasible in the hands of the immediate transferee.",source:"Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC)"},{t:"caution",text:"Textbooks and judgments written before 2010 may still state the Adorna position. Check the date of anything you read on indefeasibility, because the years in between produced decisions applying a rule that is no longer good law."},{t:"diagram",kind:"timeline",title:"Nine years of the wrong rule",caption:"Nothing in the text of the earlier judgment tells you it was wrong. This is what a citator is for, and why free Malaysian research cannot stop at the text.",alt:"Three points. The National Land Code 1965, section 340: registered title is indefeasible, subject to the exceptions in section 340(2). In 2001 Adorna Properties adopted immediate indefeasibility, so a forged transfer gave the transferee a good title. In 2010 Tan Ying Hong departed from Adorna and restored deferred indefeasibility.",events:[{year:"1965",label:"National Land Code",note:"s 340: registered title indefeasible, subject to the exceptions in s 340(2)"},{year:"2001",label:"Adorna Properties v Boonsom Boonyanit (FC)",note:"immediate indefeasibility — a forged transfer gave the transferee a good title",tone:"wrong"},{year:"2010",label:"Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San (FC)",note:"departed from Adorna; deferred indefeasibility restored",tone:"correct"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-torrens-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The Code allows a closed set of dealings — transfer, lease, charge, easement — and the vocabulary is its own.",h:"Dealings, and holding your place",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Code allows a closed set of registrable **dealings**, which are transfer, lease, charge and easement. The vocabulary is not the English one either."},{t:"p",text:"Land here is *charged* rather than mortgaged, and a **charge** is the Code's security interest over registered land. The chargee's remedies are statutory: an order for sale, or possession, on the terms the Code lays down."},{t:"p",text:"A **caveat** is an entry that freezes dealings while a claim is worked out. It creates no interest in the land at all, and what it does is stop dealings being registered while it stands. That buys the person who entered it time to prove the claim in court."},{t:"p",text:"Private, registrar's, lien-holder's and trust caveats each have their own conditions attached to them."},{t:"caution",text:"A caveat entered without a caveatable interest exposes the person who entered it to a claim for compensation. It is not a free way to block somebody else's sale."}]},{id:"sec-l-torrens-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Read section 340 itself, slowly, proviso included.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet. Read section 340 itself, slowly, and include the proviso. It is a short provision that repays being read five times over."},{t:"p",text:"Then read Tan Ying Hong, to see what the Federal Court thought had gone wrong."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qtr1",q:"What does 'title by registration' mean?",options:["Registration merely records an ownership acquired elsewhere","Ownership passes on the contract and is registered later","The register is conclusive and creates the ownership","Registration is only evidence of ownership"],answer:2,why:"A buyer deals with the register instead of investigating the history behind it.",source:"National Land Code s 340"},{id:"qtr2",q:"Why does section 6 of the Civil Law Act 1956 shut English land law out?",options:["English land law is obsolete","Land is a State matter","English doctrines of equitable interests and notice would cut across the Torrens system","The National Land Code says so"],answer:2,why:"The exclusion is what keeps the register conclusive, and conclusiveness is the whole mechanism.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956 s 6"},{id:"qtr3",q:"What is deferred indefeasibility?",options:["Every registered title is indefeasible","No registered title is safe until a year has passed","The first owner registered through fraud or forgery can be undone; a later honest buyer who paid cannot","Whether a title is safe depends on the price that was paid"],answer:2,why:"The protection is deferred to the next purchaser, who bought in good faith and for value.",source:"National Land Code s 340; Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC)"},{id:"qtr4",q:"What did Tan Ying Hong do?",options:["Introduced immediate indefeasibility","Overruled Adorna Properties and restored deferred indefeasibility","Abolished caveats","Amended section 340"],answer:1,why:"So anything written before 2010 on indefeasibility has to be checked against it.",source:"Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC), departing from Adorna Properties Sdn Bhd v Boonsom Boonyanit [2001] 1 MLJ 241 (FC)"},{id:"qtr5",q:"What is the risk of entering a caveat with no caveatable interest?",options:["Nothing, beyond having it removed","A fine under the Code","Losing the right to sue at all","Having to compensate the person you held up"],answer:3,why:"It is not a free way to block somebody else's transaction.",source:"National Land Code, the caveat provisions (ss 320-330)"}]},{id:"l-caveats",moduleId:"m08-property",title:"Dealings, charges and caveats: how an interest is protected",minutes:30,summary:"Under the Code an interest in land exists when it is registered. Everything else — contracts, caveats, liens — is machinery for getting there, or for stopping somebody else getting there first.",plants:["ms-land-01","ms-land-02","ms-land-03","ms-land-04","ms-land-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"teo-khaw-land-law",where:"the chapters on dealings, charges and caveats"},{bookId:"salleh-buang-torrens",where:"for the indefeasibility material"}],source:"National Land Code, Division IV (dealings), the charge and order-for-sale provisions, ss 320-330 on caveats and s 340 on indefeasibility; Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC); Adorna Properties Sdn Bhd v Boonsom Boonyanit [2001] 1 MLJ 241 (FC).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"The Code was revised in 2020 and section numbering must be checked against the current text before any section is cited. Check the caveat lapse period and the order-for-sale provisions in particular, and confirm both case citations in a report you can hold.",plate:"caveat",plateCaption:"A caveat struck across the register: dealings frozen while the claim behind it is worked out. It creates no interest of its own.",readMinutes:8,sections:[{id:"sec-l-caveats-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"An interest in registered land exists on registration, and not before.",h:"The register is what counts",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Devan signs the sale and purchase agreement for a shoplot in Seremban and pays the deposit. Does he own it? Not yet."},{t:"p",text:"A dealing is a transaction in registered land — a transfer, a lease, a charge. Under the Code a dealing takes effect at one particular moment, and it is not the moment of signing."},{t:"rule",text:"Any dealing under the National Land Code is effective to pass or create an interest in land only upon registration; until then the instrument passes nothing at law.",source:"National Land Code, Division IV on dealings, read with the registration provisions"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the register makes the interest. Until the transfer is registered, the signed document has given Devan nothing at law."},{t:"p",text:"The agreement does give him something. He has a contractual right, and in equity an interest capable of protection. What he does not have is title."},{t:"p",text:"Between signing and registration he is exposed. The whole of the caveat machinery exists because of that gap."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not write that a purchaser 'owns' the land on completion of the agreement. The register decides ownership. This is the mistake that unravels an entire answer on priorities."}]},{id:"sec-l-caveats-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A charge is the Code's security, and where the order for sale goes depends on the kind of title.",anchor:"fig-l-caveats-0",h:"Charges, and what a chargee may do",body:[{t:"p",text:"A bank lends RM300,000 against a house. It wants something it can sell if the loan is not repaid."},{t:"p",text:"A charge is the Code's security interest over registered land. It is created by a registered instrument, and it gives the chargee no right of possession. The security is the ability to enforce, not to occupy."},{t:"p",text:"The chargee is the lender who holds the charge. The chargor is the borrower whose land is charged."},{t:"list",items:["On default, the chargee serves the statutory notice of default. If the default is not remedied, the chargee may apply for an order for sale.","For land held under **Registry title**, the application goes to the **High Court**.","For land held under **Land Office title**, it goes to the **Land Administrator**.","The court will order a sale unless the chargor shows **cause to the contrary**. That is a narrow concept, not a general discretion to be merciful."]},{t:"caution",text:"The Registry-title and Land-Office-title routes are different tracks, with different provisions and different appeal routes. Identify which title the land is held under before you say where the application goes. Getting this wrong is the standard way to go wrong in a charge question."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Where an order for sale is applied for",caption:"Identify which title the land is held under before saying where the application goes. This is where the method marks in a charge question sit.",alt:"A branch on the kind of title. Registry title: the application goes to the High Court, which may order sale on unremedied default. Land Office title: it goes to the Land Administrator, a different track with its own provisions and appeal route.",question:"Under which title is the land held?",branches:[{cond:"Registry title",label:"The High Court",note:"order for sale on unremedied default"},{cond:"Land Office title",label:"The Land Administrator",note:"a different track, with its own provisions and appeal route"}]},{t:"checkpoint",q:"The borrower defaults. The house is held under Land Office title. Where does the bank apply for an order for sale?",options:["The Land Administrator","The High Court","The Sessions Court, because of the amount of the loan"],answer:0,why:"Land Office title goes to the Land Administrator. Registry title goes to the High Court. What decides it is the class of title, and not the size of the loan. Identify the title before you say where the application goes."}]},{id:"sec-l-caveats-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A private caveat freezes dealings while a claim is worked out; it creates no interest of its own.",h:"Private caveats",body:[{t:"p",text:"Devan hears that the seller is showing the same shoplot to another buyer. He cannot stop the seller talking. He can stop the register moving."},{t:"p",text:"Not everybody may lodge a caveat. Section 323 says who may."},{t:"rule",text:"A private caveat may be entered by a person claiming title to the land, or claiming any registrable interest in it, or claiming to be beneficially entitled under any trust affecting it.",source:"National Land Code, s 323"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you need a claim to the land itself, or to an interest in it that could be registered, or a beneficial interest under a trust. A grievance on its own is not enough."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Devan lodges a private caveat on the shoplot. What has that given him?",options:["An interest in the land, held until the sale goes through","First place in the queue, so the land must be transferred to him first","No interest at all — only a block on the register while it stands"],answer:2,reveal:"A caveat gives nothing. It stops dealings being registered, which buys time for the claim to be decided. Notice the order: you must already have a claim before you may lodge one. The caveat protects an interest and never creates one. If it felt like staking a claim on the land, that is the misreading the word invites."},{t:"p",text:"A caveat does not create an interest. It prevents the registration of dealings while it is in force, which buys time for the claim to be determined."},{t:"p",text:"It lapses after the statutory period unless it is extended. A person aggrieved by it may apply to have it removed."},{t:"p",text:"On such an application the caveator must show two things. First, that the claim discloses a serious question to be tried. Second, that the balance of convenience favours maintaining the caveat until trial."},{t:"p",text:"That is the same shape as the test for an interlocutory injunction, and it is that shape for the same reason. The court is holding the position without deciding the fight."},{t:"caution",text:"A caveat entered without reasonable cause exposes the caveator to compensation. It is not a costless holding move. Advising a client to lodge one 'just in case' is advice with a price attached."}]},{id:"sec-l-caveats-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Registrar's, lien-holder's and trust caveats — and a deposited title document points at the second.",h:"The other caveats, briefly",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Registrar's caveat** — entered by the Registrar to protect the interests of the Federation or a State, or of a person under disability, or to prevent fraud or improper dealing.","**Lien-holder's caveat** — where the issue document of title is deposited as security for a loan, this is how the lender protects it. The lien becomes enforceable once the caveat is entered.","**Trust caveat** — protecting land held on trust."]},{t:"p",text:"A question that mentions a deposited title document is almost always a lien-holder's caveat question. Spotting it by that cue is faster than reasoning from first principles."}]},{id:"sec-l-caveats-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Section 340 and deferred indefeasibility are what answer the forged-transfer worry.",h:"How this connects to indefeasibility",body:[{t:"p",text:"Registration is powerful, and that raises an obvious worry. What if somebody forges your signature and registers a transfer of your land?"},{t:"p",text:"Section 340 gives the registered proprietor an indefeasible title — a registered title that cannot be set aside. Section 340(2) then lists the exceptions, which include fraud and a void or forged instrument."},{t:"p",text:"*Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San* restored deferred indefeasibility. A person who acquires title through a void or forged instrument does not obtain an indefeasible title. A later purchaser in good faith and for value may."},{t:"caution",text:"*Adorna Properties Sdn Bhd v Boonsom Boonyanit* held the opposite, and it stood for nine years. If a source you are reading relies on it without noting that it was departed from, stop trusting that source."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qcv1",q:"When does a dealing pass an interest in registered land?",options:["On execution of the instrument","On registration","On payment of the price","On delivery of the title document"],answer:1,why:"The register creates the interest. Everything before registration is contractual or equitable only.",source:"National Land Code; an interest in registered land passes on registration"},{id:"qcv2",q:"What does a private caveat actually do?",options:["Creates an interest in the land","Transfers beneficial ownership","Prevents registration of dealings while it is in force","Operates as a charge"],answer:2,why:"It is a freeze, not an interest. It buys time for the underlying claim to be determined.",source:"National Land Code s 323"},{id:"qcv3",q:"On an application to remove a private caveat, what must the caveator show?",options:["That he will succeed at trial","That he has registered title","That the registered proprietor consents","A serious question to be tried, and that the balance of convenience favours the caveat"],answer:3,why:"The same shape as an interlocutory injunction test, because the court is holding the position, not deciding it.",source:"National Land Code, the caveat provisions (ss 320-330)"},{id:"qcv4",q:"A chargee wants an order for sale of land held under Registry title. Where does the application go?",options:["The Land Administrator","The High Court","The Sessions Court","The Registrar of Titles"],answer:1,why:"Registry title goes to the High Court. Land Office title goes to the Land Administrator.",source:"National Land Code, the charge and order-for-sale provisions"},{id:"qcv5",q:"Which case restored deferred indefeasibility under s 340?",options:["Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San","Adorna Properties v Boonsom Boonyanit","Cubic Electronics v Mars","Kepong Prospecting v Schmidt"],answer:0,why:"Tan Ying Hong departed from Adorna Properties, which had held for immediate indefeasibility.",source:"Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San [2010] 2 MLJ 1 (FC); Adorna Properties Sdn Bhd v Boonsom Boonyanit [2001] 1 MLJ 241 (FC)"},{id:"qcv6",q:"The facts mention an issue document of title deposited as security. Which caveat is in issue?",options:["A private caveat","A Registrar's caveat","A lien-holder's caveat","A trust caveat"],answer:2,why:"Deposit of the title document, plus an intention to secure, is the lien-holder's caveat cue.",source:"National Land Code, the caveat provisions (ss 320-330)"}]}];export{e as default};
